---
description: Do not cap marketing copy or headings with arbitrary max-width / ch units
alwaysApply: true
---

# No arbitrary max-width on text

Do **not** add `max-width` in `rem`, `ch`, `em`, or fixed `px` on headings, taglines, body copy, or section descriptions unless the user explicitly asks for a measure column (e.g. legal fine print, code blocks, modals with a stated width).

## Allowed

- **Page / section shells:** `max-width` on layout wrappers (e.g. `.dash`, `PageContainer`) that center the whole column — not on text nodes inside them.
- **Media:** images, video, iframes, avatars, icons (intrinsic or explicit frame sizes).
- **UI controls:** buttons, inputs, pills where width is part of the control pattern — not the prose inside marketing cards.
- **Tables / code:** `pre`, `code`, horizontal scroll regions.

## Forbidden without explicit user request

```css
/* ❌ */
.headline {
  max-width: 40rem;
}
.section__description {
  max-width: 48rem;
}
.intro {
  max-width: 65ch;
}
```

```css
/* ✅ — copy uses the parent column */
.headline {
  max-width: none;
  width: 100%;
}
```

## Headings

- Prefer natural line breaks (`text-wrap: pretty` or default) over `text-wrap: balance` on long marketing headlines — balance can force short, awkward lines that look like a fixed measure.
- Do **not** change markup or move blocks between columns to “fix” copy width unless the user asks for a layout change.

## When unsure

Use the parent flex/grid track (`minmax(0, 1fr)`) for layout — never a separate prose `max-width` to “improve readability.”